[Applause]  [Music]  this is warsaw in poland where thousands  of people have been protesting against a  near total ban on abortion  a strike is underway and major roads and  bridges have been blocked across the  country  scuffles have erupted in parliament with  opposition mps  carrying signs that say this is war  here's why the ruling has caused such  anger and why unrest there  matters  before a new high court ruling poland's  abortion laws  were already amongst the strictest in  europe abortions were only permitted in  the case of a fetal abnormality  rape incest or to protect the mother's  life  and doctors in poland can refuse to  perform legal abortions  many do so on religious grounds this has  forced thousands of women to seek  terminations abroad  or to have to risk illegal abortions i  was  like really really scared because i  didn't have money to go  up to go to abroad and i knew that there  is no other possibility for me to have  a person in medical clinic  but in october 2020 poland's top court  ruled abortion on the basis of a fetal  abnormality  is unconstitutional the overwhelming  majority of legal abortions performed in  poland  are due to fetal abnormalities so some  see this new rule  as an almost total ban women in in  poland will not be able to abort  uh pregnancies that threaten their lives  or that will  give birth to babies that will die soon  after birth in in awful pain and this is  against human rights against  women's rights and as young women we  feel that it is our obligation to stand  here today  to protest this this oppressive regime  that we have in poland  this red lightning image has become a  symbol of the protests that have  occupied several major cities  across the country and despite poland  being one of europe's most staunchly  roman catholic countries  reports suggest that the vast majority  of people are against making abortion  laws stricter  the ruling law and justice party has  effectively abolished legal abortion  without a parliamentary bill debate or  public consultation  we reject the notion that there is human  right to abortion there is  no such thing in international law there  are attempts at establishing it but  nobody ever agreed to that and this  is simply wrong this is simply  unacceptable  it's not the first time lawmakers in  poland have tried to impose a total ban  on abortion  in 2016 a similar attempt prompted black  marches  where hundreds of thousands of women  turned out in the rain to protest  forcing the government to back down  in a symbolic way this country  says us that we are nothing you know  we are not important we have to stay at  home  uh give birth and you know  do the family and care work what makes  these protests different  is that demonstrators have challenged  the authority of the church for the  first time  since the collapse of communism in 1989  the church has held  enormous influence the government  presents itself as the defender of  traditional catholic values  and has pushed back against liberal  western ideas  but now church services all over poland  have been disrupted  and priests have been confronted and  some see this as a tipping point for the  church's authority  i think there is certainly there are  certainly some groups of radical left  unfortunately they are gaining a lot of  support in  in the media recently that they are  trying to  incentivize uh yeah especially younger  people to  find a common enemy in churches of  course the very  comfortable enemy so to say but this is  very dangerous approach  we are open to to talk we're open to to  discuss the debates  uh but the civility must be  what remains to be seen is whether the  government will back down  over the issue i believe  that this is the beginning of  irish situation maybe  in you know five years we're gonna have  changes  like in ireland two years ago  the the biggest reality of abortion is  that people will always have abortion  and they will find a way  to do it

